{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwLkdr17vzjLUFWt6UHZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gbj36QgZCoyni4R7TowmVL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"find / -name figure_manifest.schema.json 2>/dev/null | head; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('paper_strategy.json'))\nfor f in d.get('figure_specs',[]): print({k:f[k] for k in f if k in('figure_id','figure_type','preferred_generator','preferred_span')})\nE\ngrep -n \"Reproducib\\|2.5.4\\|3.11.2\\|under a minute\\|under-cover\\|undercover\\|under-covers\\|0.83\\|0.89\\b\" paper.tex | head -40"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5197,"cache_read_input_tokens":49713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5197},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"48cd8476-344a-4486-9c4f-37bd4b83a91f","uuid":"82c93347-c238-4c86-8522-54b598c29e8d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T15:01:02.916Z","request_id":"req_011CgwLkdJGRb65vpSSnghwn"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gbj36QgZCoyni4R7TowmVL","type":"tool_result","content":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n{'figure_id': 'overview_schematic', 'figure_type': 'diagram', 'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'per_case_interval_score', 'figure_type': 'grouped_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'bias_and_coverage', 'figure_type': 'paired_dot_or_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'width_vs_n', 'figure_type': 'line_or_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n43:The gain is regime-dependent: the reporter only ties the naive interval under halving on real outcomes and at 600 questions, ties resampling under exhaustive search, and under-covers there (0.83--0.89).\n202:P4 & real, halv. & 200 & 24 & 0.161 & 0.165 & 0.258 & \\textbf{0.156} & 0.77 & 0.89 \\\\\n204:P6 & real, exh. & 600 & 24 & 0.096 & 0.096 & \\textbf{0.092} & 0.096 & 0.78 & 0.83 \\\\\n207:N2 & real null, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.218 & 0.168 & \\textbf{0.102} & 0.111 & 0.62 & 0.89 \\\\\n221:\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n239:\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n247:In the synthetic null N1 EB-IRT has coverage $0.971$ and interval score $0.128$ against $0.354$ for naive, whose coverage is $0.47$. In the permuted real null N2 EB-IRT has coverage $0.887$ and interval score $0.111$ (naive $0.218$, resampling $0.102$). In both nulls its bias is $0.017$: shrinkage does not reduce the estimate fully to the common mean, and the controls average $0.0173$ in absolute bias. In the constant-zero world Z1 the reporter returns exactly zero with interval $[0,0.02]$ (absolute estimate error $0$, interval score $0.020$ against $0.026$ naive and $0.059$ Bonferroni). The reporter therefore does not manufacture signal in null worlds, but its null coverage in the real world sits at the same $0.89$ as in P4.\n281:\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $47\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since the naive and resampling scores vary across seeds with standard deviations of $0.023$ and $0.025$, against $0.004$ for EB-IRT. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n283:\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ ($0.83$) and in real-outcome halving ($0.89$), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n289:After automatic prompt search, a score reported on the search sample is an optimistic and over-confident description of fresh-question accuracy: across the studied worlds the naive best-of-search estimate is too high by about 0.04 on average, and its nominal 90\\% interval contains the truth about 71\\% of the time. A searcher-agnostic empirical-Bayes latent-difficulty posterior interval, computed from the search log alone, cut the interval score from 0.220 to 0.145 in a sealed eight-seed evaluation whose qualification outcome is ``qualified'', and reduced the bias by about two thirds. The improvement concentrates in synthetic worlds, at small question budgets, and under halving relative to resampling; for real exhaustive search a question-resampling correction is equally good, and for real halving and large question budgets the new reporter is no better than the naive interval and under-covers. We therefore recommend the reporter as a conditional, searcher-agnostic default for small samples, together with the evaluation protocol: known truth, null and constant controls, coverage next to interval score, and seed-level uncertainty. Open questions are whether the optimism alters rankings, how much of the coverage is owed to the width calibration, and whether the pattern holds for stronger models and real prompt optimizers.\n385:\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n386:All numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"48cd8476-344a-4486-9c4f-37bd4b83a91f","uuid":"4ffb1e6d-83ca-46d6-973c-28d7a3d232b7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T15:01:03.111Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n{'figure_id': 'overview_schematic', 'figure_type': 'diagram', 'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'per_case_interval_score', 'figure_type': 'grouped_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'bias_and_coverage', 'figure_type': 'paired_dot_or_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n{'figure_id': 'width_vs_n', 'figure_type': 'line_or_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'preferred_span': 'full_width'}\n43:The gain is regime-dependent: the reporter only ties the naive interval under halving on real outcomes and at 600 questions, ties resampling under exhaustive search, and under-covers there (0.83--0.89).\n202:P4 & real, halv. & 200 & 24 & 0.161 & 0.165 & 0.258 & \\textbf{0.156} & 0.77 & 0.89 \\\\\n204:P6 & real, exh. & 600 & 24 & 0.096 & 0.096 & \\textbf{0.092} & 0.096 & 0.78 & 0.83 \\\\\n207:N2 & real null, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.218 & 0.16