ully to the common mean, and the controls average $0.0173$ in absolute bias. In the constant-zero world Z1 the reporter returns exactly zero with interval $[0,0.02]$ (absolute estimate error $0$, interval score $0.020$ against $0.026$ naive and $0.059$ Bonferroni). The reporter therefore does not manufacture signal in null worlds, but its null coverage in the real world sits at the same $0.89$ as in P4.\n281:\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $47\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since the naive and resampling scores vary across seeds with standard deviations of $0.023$ and $0.025$, against $0.004$ for EB-IRT. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n283:\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that