nd large question budgets the new reporter is no better than the naive interval and under-covers. We therefore recommend the reporter as a conditional, searcher-agnostic default for small samples, together with the evaluation protocol: known truth, null and constant controls, coverage next to interval score, and seed-level uncertainty. Open questions are whether the optimism alters rankings, how much of the coverage is owed to the width calibration, and whether the pattern holds for stronger models and real prompt optimizers.\n385:\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n386:All numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
