{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015uzSJsS2xPatQVu3gwUUrp","type":"tool_result","content":"\\begin{abstract}\nAutomatic prompt and pipeline search reuses one benchmark sample both to choose a configuration and to score it, so the reported score can overstate accuracy on fresh questions.\nWe measure this optimism in controlled worlds with known truth---synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct on MMLU-Pro under 24 prompt configurations, searched exhaustively or by successive halving---and test a searcher-agnostic remedy.\nThe naive best-of-search score overstates fresh-question accuracy by 0.037 on average (about 0.05 at 60 benchmark questions), and its nominal 90\\% Wilson interval contains the fresh accuracy in only 71\\% of replications.\nThe remedy fits an empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model to the full search log and reports posterior quantiles.\nIn a sealed evaluation over eight seeds it lowers the mean 90\\%-level interval score to 0.145, against 0.220 (naive), 0.197 (Bonferroni-widened Wilson) and 0.246 (question resampling), with coverage 0.929; the evaluator's qualification outcome is ``qualified''.\nThe gain is regime-dependent: the reporter only ties the naive interval under halving on real outcomes and at 600 questions, ties resampling under exhaustive search, and under-covers there (0.83--0.89).\nThe evidence covers one small model, one benchmark and a hand-set width calibration, and does not test effects on ranking decisions.\n\\end{abstract}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.resample_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage]]\n129:\\textbf{Aggregation and uncertainty.} Per-case values average replications; the primary aggregate averages P1--P2 (synthetic, weight 0.4) and P3--P6 (real, weight 0.6) within family. The sealed evaluation uses eight seeds (9101--9108), each running all nine cases. We report mean $\\pm$ standard deviation of seed-level values and, for reporter differences, paired seed-level differences. The evaluator records interval score, bias, coverage and width for the EB-IRT reporter and interval score and bias for the baselines; for baseline coverage and width we replayed the evaluator's own deterministic world, search and baseline code, which reproduced all recorded baseline interval scores and biases exactly (maximum absolute difference 0), and report the derived coverage and width. Per-case records carry an unverified diagnostics label from the evaluator, so we treat aggregate metrics as authoritative and per-case values as supporting. The reporter was frozen before the sealed evaluation, and the evaluation completed all replications (completed fraction 1.0).\n153:\\caption{Sealed final evaluation, primary cases P1--P6 (0.4 synthetic / 0.6 real), mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over eight seeds. The interval score is at level 0.90 (lower is better); coverage should be near 0.90. Bonferroni-Wilson shares the naive point estimate. Baseline coverage and width are replayed from the evaluator's code (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).}\n228:\\caption{Diagnostics for the primary cases (mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds). (a) Signed bias of the point estimate for the naive and EB-IRT reporters. (b) Empirical coverage of the 90\\% intervals; dashed line marks the nominal level. Naive coverage is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n235:\\caption{Mean interval width on the real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) as the number of benchmark questions grows (mean over eight seeds). EB-IRT is wider than the naive interval by a factor of 1.1--1.3 at each $N$ and much narrower than Bonferroni-Wilson. Naive and Bonferroni widths are replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n338:\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), width and signed bias for EB-IRT and the naive reporter. Naive width is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n362:\\caption{Bonferroni-Wilson and resampling reporters per case: interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), and replayed coverage and width.}\n386:All numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"48cd8476-344a-4486-9c4f-37bd4b83a91f","uuid":"a38775ca-248d-45bc-8323-b102834e344e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T15:01:05.667Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\begin{abstract}\nAutomatic prompt and pipeline search reuses one benchmark sample both to choose a configuration and to score it, so the reported score can overstate accuracy on fresh questions.\nWe measure this optimism in controlled worlds with known truth---synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct on MMLU-Pro under 24 prompt configurations, searched exhaustively or by successive halving---and test a searcher-agnostic remedy.\nThe naive best-of-search score overstates fresh-question accuracy by 0.037 on average (about 0.05 at 60 benchmark questions), and its nominal 90\\% Wilson interval contains the fresh accuracy in only 71\\% of replications.\nThe remedy fits an empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model to the full search log and reports posterior quantiles.\nIn a sealed evaluation over eight seeds it lowers the mean 90\\%-level interval score to 0.145, against 0.220 (naive), 0.197 (Bonferroni-widened Wilson) and 0.246 (question resampling), with coverage 0.929; the evaluator's qualification outcome is ``qualified''.\nThe gain is regime-dependent: the reporter only ties the naive interval under halving on real outcomes and at 600 questions, ties resampling under exhaustive search, and under-covers there (0.83--0.89).\nThe evidence covers one small model, one benchmark and a hand-set width calibration, and does not test effects on ranking decisions.\n\\end{abstract}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.resample_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage]]\n129:\\textbf{Aggregation and uncertainty.} Per-case values average replications; the primary aggregate averages P1--P2 (synthetic, weight 0.4) and P3--P6 (real, weight 0.6) within family. The sealed evaluation uses eight seeds (9101--9108), each running all nine cases. We report mean $\\pm$ standard deviation of seed-level values and, for reporter differences, paired seed-level differences. The evaluator records interval score, bias, coverage and width for the EB-IRT reporter and interval score and bias for the baselines; for baseline coverage and width we replayed the evaluator's own deterministic world, search and baseline code, which reproduced all recorded baseline interval scores and biases exactly (maximum absolute difference 0), and report the derived coverage and width. Per-case records carry an unverified diagnostics label from the evaluator, so we treat aggregate metrics as authoritative and per-case values as supporting. The reporter was frozen before the sealed evaluation, and the evaluation completed all replications (completed fraction 1.0).\n153:\\caption{Sealed final evaluation, primary cases P1--P6 (0.4 synthetic / 0.6 real), mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over eight seeds. The interval score is at level 0.90 (lower is better); coverage should be near 0.90. Bonferroni-Wilson shares the naive point estimate. Baseline coverage and width are replayed from the evaluator's code (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).}\n228:\\caption{Diagnostics for the primary cases (mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds). (a) Signed bias of the point estimate for the naive and EB-IRT reporters. (b) Empirical coverage of the 90\\% intervals; dashed line marks the nominal level. Naive coverage is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n235:\\caption{Mean interval width on the real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) as the number of benchmark questions grows (mean over eight seeds). EB-IRT is wider than the naive interval by a factor of 1.1--1.3 at each $N$ and much narrower than Bonferroni-Wilson. Naive and Bonferroni widths are replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n338:\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), width and signed bias for EB-IRT and the naive reporter. Naive width is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n362:\\caption{Bonferroni-Wilson and resampling reporters per case: interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), and replayed coverage and width.}\n386:All numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
